#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "cd4ebf12-7e4c-5110-872e-c381ea9b2646" :question/math-academy-id "e-8712" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n$p_1(t)=t+1, \\qquad p_2(t)=t, \\qquad p_3(t)=t-1$.\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}_1[t]$ of polynomials of degree $1$ or less in the variable $t$ is defined as\n$\\langle p(t),q(t) \\rangle =p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1)$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $p_2(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{p_1(t), p_2(t), p_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n$⟨p_{1}(t), p_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨t + 1, t - 1⟩; = (t + 1)\\begin{vmatrix}_{-1} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{-1} + (t + 1) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1}; = (-1 + 1) \\cdot (-1 - 1) + (1 + 1) \\cdot (1 - 1); = 0$,\nwhich means that $p_1(t) \\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement II is false since\n$⟨p_{2}(t), p_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨t, t - 1⟩; = (t)\\begin{vmatrix}_{-1} \\cdot (t - 1) & _{-1} + (t) & _{1} \\cdot (t - 1)\\end{vmatrix}_{1}; = (-1) \\cdot (-1 - 1) + (1) \\cdot (1 - 1); = 2 \\ne 0$,\nwhich means that $p_2(t) \\not\\perp p_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement III is false. Since $p_2(t) \\not\\perp p_3(t)$, the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "30795f8b-9c50-5a61-9b08-385b0794c4e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8713" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is defined as\n$\\langle \\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 2x_3 y_3$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n$⟨v_{1}, v_{2}⟩|= 3(2)(-1) + (2)(1) + 2(1)(2); =-6 + 2 + 4; = 0$,\nwhich means that $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{v}_2$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement II is false since\n$⟨v_{1}, v_{3}⟩|= 3(2)(0) + (2)(1) + 2(1)(1); = 0 + 2 + 2; = 4 \\ne 0$,\nwhich means that $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf{v}_3$, the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/id #uuid "dd873dd6-fdf6-50af-a080-abbb1800a096" :question/math-academy-id "e-8714" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n$f_1(t)=1, \\qquad f_2(t)=t, \\qquad f_3(t)=2t+1$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $\\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1]$ is defined as\n$\\langle f(t),g(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^{1} f(t)g(t) \\: \\text{d}t$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_1(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $f_2(t) \\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S=\\{f_1(t), f_2(t), f_3(t) \\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n$⟨f_{1}(t), f_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨1, 2t + 1⟩; = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2t + 1)dt; = (t^{2} + t) \\mid _{-1}^{1}; = 1^{2} + 1 - ((-1)^{2} + (-1)); = 2 \\ne 0$.\nThis means that $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement II is false. We have\n$⟨f_{2}(t), f_{3}(t)⟩|= ⟨t, 2t + 1⟩; = ∫_{-1}^{1}t(2t + 1)dt; = ∫_{-1}^{1}(2t^{2} + t)dt; = \\frac{2t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{t^{2}}{2} \\mid _{-1}^{1}; = (2(1)^{3})/(3) + \\frac{1^{2}}{2} - ((2(-1)^{3})/(3) + ((-1)^{2})/(2)); = \\frac{7}{6} + \\frac{1}{6}; = \\frac{4}{3} \\ne 0$.\nThis means that $f_2(t) \\not\\perp f_3(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n- Statement III is false. Since $f_1(t) \\not\\perp f_2(t)$, the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67683e7d-bf4f-5fdf-8fe5-df996c30e836" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Vectors Is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80f89e46-abe3-5e56-b109-35305d50ebb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133323} {:answer/id #uuid "8d842035-20a3-5c17-bbed-86405855e4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133324} {:answer/id #uuid "063313fc-4c60-5a52-a7c4-fa8932c95614" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133325} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0f8569-cef8-5979-ab1c-6f5a0051b792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133326} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9ff11d-fde6-5e1b-8d2f-9ae29f9ecc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f89e46-abe3-5e56-b109-35305d50ebb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133323} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca6bdf87-6562-58e3-8959-55e521a6dc84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133322}] :question/id #uuid "89d04c73-ac43-52ba-9b0d-f07e5d286354" :question/math-academy-id "q-98523" :question/problem "$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the vectors shown above. Given that the inner product in the vector space $R^{3}$ is defined as\n$⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + 2x_{2}y_{2} + 3x_{3}y_{3}$,\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v_{1}⊥v_{2}$ with respect to the given inner product\n2. $v_{1}⊥v_{3}$ with respect to the given inner product\n3. $S = {v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product"}]}
